<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balaji Vishwanath</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1662">1662</a>–<a href="/years/1720">1720</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Peshwa Balaji Vishwanath</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balaji Vishwanath was the first of the hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwas</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> state, serving from 1713 to 1720. He helped Shahu secure the Maratha throne and negotiated recognition from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> of Maratha rights to collect revenue in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. He established the Peshwa's office as the centre of Maratha power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balaji-vishwanath.jpg" alt="Balaji Vishwanath" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amit20081980 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:His_Highness_Balaji_Vishwanath_Peshwa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Balaji Vishwanath Bhat was born on 1 January 1662 into a Konkanastha Chitpavan Brahmin family that held the hereditary office of Deshmukh of Shrivardhan under the Siddi of Janjira. According to the Purandare daftar he came up to the Desh before 1692, serving Ambajipant Purandare of Saswad and later Ramchandrapant Amatya and Shankrajipant Sachiv. He was Sarsubhedar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> from 1699 to 1702 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulatabad">Daulatabad</a> from 1703 to 1707, joined Senapati Dhanaji Jadhav at the siege of Sinhagad in 1703, and in 1708 was named Senakarte, the maker of armies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s death in 1707 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> released Shahu, the son of Sambhaji, and a civil war followed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarabai">Tarabai</a>, the widow of Rajaram. Balaji sided with Shahu against the Senapati Chandrasen Jadhav, and in 1712 helped bring about Tarabai's fall at Kolhapur, where Rajaram's other widow Rajasbai installed her son Sambhaji II. Sent against the admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanhoji-angre">Kanhoji Angre</a>, who had defeated and imprisoned the Peshwa Bahiroji Pingale, Balaji negotiated instead at Lonavala and won him over to Shahu. Shahu then dismissed Pingale and made Balaji Peshwa on 16 November 1713.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In July 1718 Balaji agreed a treaty with Sayyid Hussain Ali Khan, the Mughal viceroy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> were to have <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauth">chauth</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardeshmukhi">sardeshmukhi</a> of the Deccan provinces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, in return for nominal Mughal overlordship and 15,000 horsemen. When Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhsiyar">Farrukhsiyar</a> refused to ratify it, Balaji marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> with Hussain Ali Khan and Maratha cavalry under Parsoji Bhosale; Farrukhsiyar was deposed, and his successor Rafi-ul-Darjat ratified the treaty in 1719. Balaji also brought back Shahu's mother Yesubai from captivity. That year he defeated and killed Yashwantrao Thorat at the Battle of Panhala. He retired to Saswad in October 1719 and died on 12 April 1720; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a> succeeded him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Balaji Vishwanath” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Peshwa Balaji Vishwanath</b> (1713–1720) and his son Baji Rao I (1720–1740) drove expansion north into Malwa, Gujarat and Bundelkhand, defeating the Nizam at Palkhed (1728) and Bhopal (1737) and raiding the outskirts of Delhi in 1737.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Shrivardhan, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Saswad, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.03,73.02&amp;year=1699" aria-label="Shrivardhan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Balaji Vishwanath"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6049 5956h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6049 5956h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.03,73.02&amp;year=1699">What was here in 1699? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
